qjs.wasm and qjswasm/ are v0.0.6 plus the C changes in items 16 to 18. The
upstream *_test.go files were not copied (they need testify); fork_test.go,
audit_regression_test.go and audit_cglue_test.go cover the changes below.

Changes made for Flats (search for "FORK (flats)"):

1. runtime.go: wazero >= 1.12 binds function type IDs to one runtime's store,
   so upstream (which compiled qjs.wasm once in a throwaway runtime and
   instantiated it in every new runtime) fails with "signature mismatch" on
   wazero v1.12.0. Every Runtime now compiles qjs.wasm inside its own wazero
   runtime, through a process-wide shared wazero.CompilationCache (or a
   per-directory file cache when Option.CacheDir is set), so only the first
   compile per configuration is slow.
2. runtime.go: the wazero RuntimeConfig is built per New. CloseOnContextDone
   is therefore a per-runtime setting; upstream cached the config of the first
   New in the process, so a first runtime without CloseOnContextDone silently
   disabled timeouts for every later one.
3. options.go/runtime.go: new Option.MemoryLimitPages caps the wasm linear
   memory (64 KiB pages; 0 means DefaultMemoryLimitPages = 1024 = 64 MiB).
   QuickJS's own MemoryLimit does not bound every allocation.
4. options.go/runtime.go: Option.CWD (default os.Getwd(), mounted read-write
   at "/") was replaced by Option.ReadOnlyDir: no file system at all by
   default, or one host directory mounted read-only at "/".
5. options.go: Stdout/Stderr default to io.Discard instead of the host
   process's os.Stdout/os.Stderr.
6. runtime.go: Close also closes the per-runtime wazero runtime.
7. stack.go (new) + runtime.go: qjs.wasm's 64 KiB C stack sits directly above
   its static data and QuickJS's stack check is compiled out under WASI, so
   ~250 levels of JS recursion silently corrupted the runtime. The module
   bytes are patched in memory to export the only global (__stack_pointer,
   verified to be the single mutable i32 global), and each Runtime moves its
   stack into a dedicated malloc'd block (Option.StackSize, default 1 MiB)
   whose bottom 64 KiB is a canary; Runtime.StackIntact reports whether a call
   reached it. qjs.wasm itself is not modified on disk.
8. runtime.go: guest sleeps (WASI poll_oneoff clock waits behind os.sleep,
   os.setTimeout and the std event loop) use a context-aware sleep that
   returns when Option.Context is done. Upstream used wazero's host sleep,
   which ignores the context, so os.sleep(3600000) blocked a call for an hour
   regardless of CloseOnContextDone.
9. runtime.go: WASI random_get reads crypto/rand instead of wazero's default
   fixed-seed source, which returns the same bytes in every runtime.

Usage note (unchanged upstream behaviour): every wasm call passes the
Runtime's *Context (which embeds a context.Context) to wazero, and with
CloseOnContextDone wazero reads ctx.Done() from a per-call goroutine that may
outlive the call. Assigning Runtime.Context().Context between calls is
therefore a data race; pass a stable Option.Context whose Done/Err delegate to
an atomically swapped per-request context instead (internal/runtime does).
10. Go binding ownership: release JS C strings with exported JS_FreeCString;
    free temporary input handles, atoms, callback argument clones and awaited
    promises; copy borrowed ArrayBuffer stores without freeing them. Avoid
    upstream QJS_JSONStringify's freed pointer and property enumeration's
    unexported js_free path using JS-owned results. No wasm rebuild is needed.
11. Capture JSON.stringify and Reflect enumeration intrinsics before guest code
    can mutate globals. Enumeration cleans partial results and skips properties
    removed by Proxy traps; ForEach releases borrowed keys/values after each
    callback, including panic paths. Go-converted functions retain one owned
    reference per distinct JS function until Runtime.Close, so they survive
    borrowed callback cleanup. Converted Go functions must not be invoked after
    closing their runtime. Array conversions release their borrowed inputs too.
    Reflect enumeration returns public string/symbol keys; VM-internal private
    atoms are not exposed through this binding.
12. Runtime.Close always closes the native wazero runtime and clears handles,
    even when timeout cancellation makes JS teardown fail. The original teardown
    panic remains observable, and a subsequent Close is safe. ForEach propagates
    throwing getters after clearing their pending JS exception and owned values.
13. clock.go (new), nanotime_unix.go/nanotime_windows.go (new), runtime.go:
    every runtime has its own guest clocks (ModuleConfig WithWalltime/
    WithNanotime) instead of wazero's system clocks. Wall time is time.Now
    as before; the monotonic clock is the host's (runtime.nanotime, what
    WithSysNanotime reads; a package-local base on Windows) plus a
    per-runtime offset that is 0 for every fresh runtime. Readings handed to
    the guest while New_QJS runs are recorded to locate JSContext's
    random_state. Option.Census counts clock reads, output bytes and file
    opens/closes of the ReadOnlyDir mount (tests only; with it the mount is
    the same read-only DirFS through a counting wrapper).
14. image.go (new), runtime.go, options.go: runtime images. Runtime.Image
    captures linear memory (the 64 KiB pages that differ from a freshly
    instantiated module), __stack_pointer and the Go-side wrappers (handles,
    global object, intrinsics, relocated stack, proxy registry kinds, caller
    raw values); New(Option{Image, ImageFunctions}) instantiates the module,
    writes the image instead of calling New_QJS, reseeds random_state from
    crypto/rand, continues the captured monotonic clock and re-registers the
    registry in order. Images are keyed (format, SHA-256 of the patched
    qjs.wasm, memory-shaping options, mount presence, Option.ImageBinding)
    and serialized with a CRC-32C. Capture is refused unless, over the
    runtime's whole life, the caller never called Runtime.Taint, the guest
    wrote no output, random_state is unchanged, no Go-converted JS function
    is retained, the registry holds exactly ids 1..n of Go functions and the
    runtime's context, and the stack canary is intact. New's output writers
    are wrapped to count bytes.
15. shell.go (new), runtime.go: NewShell builds the flat-independent part of
    New (wazero runtime, WASI, host module, compiled module) ahead of time;
    New(Option{Shell}) consumes it once when the runtime-wide options match.
    New also no longer dereferences its nil named result on error paths
    (before, those errors became a recovered nil-pointer panic and the
    wazero runtime leaked).
16. qjswasm/helpers.c, qjs.wasm: QJS_IsInstanceOf returns false and clears
    the pending exception when JS_IsInstanceOf fails (-1 used to read as
    true, so Map/Set/ArrayBuffer detection classified throwing objects as
    instances and left the context poisoned). detect_entry_point frees each
    suffix candidate that does not exist. qjs.wasm is rebuilt from these
    sources with the pinned quickjs-ng d01ca44, clang 23 with the WASI
    wasm32-wasip1 sysroot, and wasm-opt -O3; the build is not byte-identical
    to upstream's wasi-sdk 20 binary.

17. qjswasm/eval.c: the bytecode path handed its function object to
    JS_EvalFunction, which consumes it, and then freed it again when
    evaluation threw. The first throwing bytecode Eval corrupted the guest
    heap and the next call trapped. Loading non-module bytecode as a module
    leaked the compiled module. A resolved entry point is now owned by its
    caller (detect_entry_point leaked one allocation per file evaluation and
    per module import), and a module the loader cannot resolve throws a
    ReferenceError instead of returning a null module with no exception.
18. qjswasm/helpers.c: QJS_ToInt64, QJS_ToInt32, QJS_ToUint32 and
    QJS_ToFloat64 clear the pending exception of a failed conversion, so
    converting a symbol or an object with a throwing valueOf no longer
    poisons the context (the next, unrelated Eval used to fail with the stale
    TypeError). QJS_ToEpochTime reports NaN and clears the exception when a
    Date's getTime throws or returns a non-number, so a hostile Date reads as
    invalid instead of 1970. QJS_ToCString and QJS_AtomToCString pack the
    real string length instead of strlen, so a JS string or property key with
    U+0000 reaches Go whole instead of being truncated at the NUL;
    QJS_NewStringLen (new export) sets a string from an explicit byte length.
    QJS_JSONStringify keeps the string it returns (the caller frees it with
    JS_FreeCString; it used to free it before returning),
    QJS_GetOwnPropertyNames no longer returns VM-private atoms, every
    packed-pointer helper throws an out-of-memory exception instead of
    returning NULL when its wrapper allocation fails, and init of the
    QJS_PROXY_VALUE prototype frees the prototype object it hands to the
    constructor.
19. qjswasm/, scripts/build-qjs-wasm.sh, Makefile: `make qjs-wasm` rebuilds
    qjs.wasm from the pinned quickjs-ng
    d01ca4491fb24ccfeccb4c7394e28a3b21fd5986 with Homebrew llvm, wasi-libc,
    wasi-runtimes and wasm-opt -O3. The script copies the glue from
    internal/qjs/qjswasm/, fails with a clear message when a tool is missing,
    and reproduces the shipped binary byte for byte (it configures with
    -DQJS_BUILD_LIBC=ON, which compiles quickjs-libc.c with the _GNU_SOURCE
    the quickjs build sets, and -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5).
